{{also|bonâ fide}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[bonâ fide]]

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˌbəʊ.nəˈfaɪ.di/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˌboʊnəˈfaɪdi/|/ˈboʊnə.faɪd/|/ˈboʊnəˌfiːdeɪ/}}
* {{audio|en-us-bona-fide 2.ogg|Audio (US)}}, {{audio|en-us-bona-fide.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|En-us-bona fide.ogg|Audio (US)}}

====Usage notes====
The pronunciation {{IPA|/ˈboʊnə.faɪd/}}, although very widespread (especially in the US), is regarded by some as incorrect.<ref>Webster's Third New International Dictionary, Unabridged</ref>

===Etymology===
From the {{etyl|la}} {{term|bona|bonā|lang=la}} {{term|fide|fidē|lang=la}} (“in good faith”) which is the {{ablative}} of {{term|bona|lang=la}} {{term|fides|fidēs|lang=la}} (“good faith”).

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# Done in [[good]] [[faith]].
#: ''Although he failed, the prime minister made a '''bona fide''' attempt to repair the nation's damaged economy.''
# [[genuine|Genuine]]; not [[counterfeit]].
#: ''This is a '''bona fide''' Roman coin.''
#* '''2000''', ''[http://www.imdb.com/title/tt0190590/quotes O Brother Where Art Thou?]'' (movie):
#*: Ulysses Everett McGill: I am the only daddy you got! I’m the damn pater familias!
#*: Wharvey Gal: But you ain’t '''bona fide'''!

====Usage notes====
Sometimes misspelled as *''bonafied,'' by incorrectly analyzing as the past tense of assumed *''bonafy''.<ref>[http://www.wsu.edu/~brians/errors/bonafied.html Bonafied / Bona Fide], Paul Brians</ref>

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|done in good faith}} [[sincere]]
* {{sense|genuine}} [[authentic]], [[genuine]]

====Antonyms====
* {{sense|done in good faith}} [[mala fide]]

